<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Whether this build was signed with the iCloud container, and therefore
	     whether `CloudKitZone.isEnabled` may hand out the real transports.

	     It lives in a physical file rather than in `INFOPLIST_KEY_…` because
	     that mechanism only injects keys Xcode already knows about: a custom
	     one is dropped, silently, with the build still reporting success. The
	     setting sat in the project reading `INFOPLIST_KEY_DiThoiCloudKitEnabled
	     = NO` and the key was not in the built Info.plist at all — so
	     `isEnabled` was answering off a key that could never exist, and turning
	     it to YES would have changed nothing. `actool` dropping sixteen icon
	     renditions with exit code 0, one build phase over.

	     Everything else the app needs from Info.plist is still generated:
	     `GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE` merges into this file rather than replacing
	     it, which is the same arrangement the widget target already has.

	     Turning it to `<true/>` is the last step of switching CloudKit on — the
	     entitlement has to be real first, or the app asks `CKContainer` for a
	     container it was not signed for.

	     Note for anyone editing with `plutil`: it rewrites the file and drops
	     every comment here. Edit it by hand. -->
	<key>DiThoiCloudKitEnabled</key>
	<false/>

	<!-- Without this, tapping a `CKShare` link does not open the app at all —
	     iOS hands it to the browser and the invitation dead-ends on a web page
	     about iCloud. It is what makes the system deliver the share to
	     `AppDelegate.application(_:userDidAcceptCloudKitShareWith:)` — the app
	     delegate and not a scene delegate, for the reason written down in
	     `ShareAcceptance.swift` — which is the only way into
	     `TripStore.acceptInvitation`, and that is the only thing that moves a
	     `tripID` between two phones.

	     It is on regardless of `DiThoiCloudKitEnabled`, and that is deliberate:
	     the flag decides whether this build can *act* on an invitation, and a
	     build that cannot should still be able to say so out loud. Silently not
	     opening looks like a broken link on the sender's side. -->
	<key>CKSharingSupported</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- The day widget's link into tonight's page, `dithoi://tonight`
	     (`QuickAction.tonightURL`). A widget can only open its app by URL, and
	     without a registered scheme `widgetURL` does nothing at all. The link
	     carries no trip data, and `RootView` drops it on any phone that has no
	     tonight to write. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.lovedones.dithoi</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>dithoi</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
